    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Many property insurance disputes are not about coverage at all. The insurer agrees that wind tore off part of the roof or that hail damaged the metal panels; the disagreement is about how much of the roof, which materials, and at what price. Insurance appraisal in Corpus Christi is a process designed for exactly that kind of disagreement. Coastal Bend property owners should know that there are two versions: the appraisal clause written into most private property policies, and the statutory appraisal process that applies to TWIA windstorm claims. This page explains both. It is general information, not legal advice, and your policy or the statute controls.</p>

<h2>Policy Appraisal Clauses</h2>
<p>Most Texas homeowners and commercial property policies include an appraisal clause. The wording varies, but the structure is usually similar:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A disagreement on the amount of loss.</strong> The parties have exchanged estimates and cannot agree.</li>
<li><strong>A written demand.</strong> Either the policyholder or the insurer demands appraisal in writing, following the policy's procedure.</li>
<li><strong>Each side selects an appraiser.</strong> Policies usually require a competent and impartial (or disinterested) appraiser, named within a set number of days.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers select an umpire.</strong> If they cannot agree, many policies allow either party to ask a court to appoint one.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers evaluate the loss.</strong> They inspect the property, review estimates and documents, and try to agree.</li>
<li><strong>An award is issued.</strong> If the appraisers agree, they sign the award. If not, they submit their differences to the umpire, and an award agreed by any two of the three sets the amount.</li>
</ol>
<p>Under typical clauses, each side pays its own appraiser and the umpire's fee is shared. After the award, the insurer usually applies the deductible, prior payments, and any depreciation terms in the policy.</p>

<h2>TWIA Statutory Appraisal</h2>
<p>For windstorm and hail claims insured by the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association, appraisal is set by statute rather than by a policy clause alone. Texas Insurance Code §2210.574, part of Chapter 2210, Subchapter L-1, provides a specific process for disputes over the amount of a TWIA claim. Under the <a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.2210.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">statute</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li>The claimant may demand appraisal within <strong>60 days</strong> after receiving TWIA's notice accepting the claim in full or in part.</li>
<li>TWIA may grant <strong>30 more days</strong> if the claimant asks in writing, with good cause, within 15 days after the 60-day period ends.</li>
<li>The claimant and TWIA <strong>split appraisal costs equally</strong>.</li>
<li>If the two appraisers cannot agree on an umpire, the <strong>Commissioner of Insurance selects one from a roster kept by TDI</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The 60-day window is short, and it starts when you receive the acceptance or partial-acceptance notice, not when repairs begin. If you disagree with TWIA's number, note that date right away. Our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> can help you estimate it, and the <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page covers the broader TWIA process. TWIA appraisal addresses the amount of an accepted claim; disputes over a denial of coverage follow a separate notice, alternative dispute resolution, and lawsuit process under the same subchapter.</p>

<h2>Policy Appraisal vs. TWIA Appraisal at a Glance</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Typical policy appraisal clause</th><th>TWIA statutory appraisal</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Source of the rules</td><td>The policy's appraisal clause</td><td>Tex. Ins. Code §2210.574</td></tr>
<tr><td>What it decides</td><td>Amount of loss, not coverage</td><td>Amount of an accepted or partially accepted claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time to demand</td><td>Set by the policy, if at all</td><td>60 days after receiving the acceptance notice, with a possible 30-day extension</td></tr>
