<h2>When Appraisal May Make Sense</h2>
<p>Appraisal tends to fit best when the remaining dispute is narrow and measurable: whether a hail-damaged roof should be repaired or replaced, how many squares of material are needed, whether unit prices reflect Corpus Christi labor and material costs after a storm, or how much drying and rebuild work a water loss requires. It tends to fit poorly when the insurer has denied coverage outright, when a policy exclusion is squarely at issue, or when the gap between estimates is small compared with the cost of appraisers and an umpire. If the claim was denied, see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> instead.</p>
<p>Before anyone sends a demand, it helps to list each disputed item, attach the evidence for it, and confirm that the disagreement really is about amount. Sometimes a well-supported supplement or reinspection closes the gap without appraisal; our pages on <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> describe those routes. As a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster, we generally treat appraisal as one option among several rather than an automatic next step.</p>

<h2>Documentation That Supports an Appraisal</h2>
<ul>
<li>The full policy, including the appraisal clause and endorsements, or for TWIA claims the acceptance notice and its date</li>
<li>The insurer's estimate or estimates</li>
<li>A detailed line-item estimate of your own, such as one prepared in Xactimate</li>
<li>Photos, measurements, drone images, and any engineering or moisture reports</li>
<li>Contractor bids and invoices that support local pricing</li>
<li>Correspondence and payment letters showing which items are in dispute</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> covers the core items, and an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> is often the right first step to identify the differences.</p>

<h2>Pros, Cons, and Common Issues</h2>
<p>Appraisal is often faster and less formal than litigation, and it is decided by people with construction and claims knowledge. On the other hand, it usually does not resolve coverage disputes, it has real costs, and the award may come in lower than you hoped. Disputes about appraiser impartiality, delays in agreeing on an umpire, and awards that do not separate categories (such as building versus contents, or replacement cost versus actual cash value) can create further problems. Appraisal is different from a lawsuit, which can address coverage and statutory claims and requires a Texas attorney. For non-TWIA claims, Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542A requires written notice at least 61 days before filing many property suits; that pre-suit notice does not apply to actions against TWIA.</p>

<h2>Appraisal Experience From More Than One Side</h2>
<p>Joseph Dittman, the licensed Texas public adjuster behind Corpus Christi Public Adjuster, is also a certified insurance appraiser and insurance umpire, with a background that includes carrier-side adjusting, public adjusting, and appraisal work. That perspective helps when we explain how appraisal panels typically evaluate estimates and what documentation they look for. We will talk with you about whether appraisal fits your claim, the costs involved, and who should serve as your appraiser under your policy's requirements.</p>
<p>We cannot guarantee an appraisal result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If you are weighing appraisal, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> and bring your estimates and any acceptance notice.</p>
